Well said!

Penny Arcade recently had some good commentary on this subject.  They
were complaining about how the sequel to Prince of Persia seems to
have been designed to address the concerns of those who hated the
previous version of the game.

Of course, this raises the question of what the game's object is :>

I do, however, believe that the dev team should not think of everyone.
 I'm even relatively certain they aren't thinking of everyone.  Past
pudding farming style exploits usually were closed off (polypiling,
sink kicking, etc).  The fact people enjoyed free polypiling didn't
stop them from introducing golems.

It may be arrogant to make a game change to exclude a playstyle that
one sees as degenerate.  However, I would contend it is *necessary* to
game design to make such changes.  The real risk is that the
degenerate game styles become common place and end up commandeering
control of the games evolution.  Content becomes balanced against the
degenerate behaviour, forcing those remaining true to the original
playstyle to also adapt or quit.

One would think I am advocating the rail-roading of playstyle or of
gameplay.  It is rather the opposite.  The more open you want your
game to be to different playstyles, the more careful you must be about
the edge cases.  Otherwise, the players will find the "key" pattern,
exploit it, and leave the vast majority of your work untouched.  The
players farming for their elusive spellbook aren't attempting other
paths: such as, say, ascending without the necessary spellbook.

I do agree with the original poster that the right place to address
this concern is with Death Drop.  Death Drop is an unfortunate
hangover.  Item creation on death doesn't mix well with monster
inventory systems, IMHO.
